\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Elisa Robot Charging and Storage}

One challenge when working with 100 Elisa robots is to charge and
store the robots efficiently. Originally, the available charging
station could hold only three robots at a time.  Three charging
stations were available, which meant that  nine robots could be
charged using the stations at once. The robots also had to be pushed
onto the charging contacts, and the spring-loaded contact pins could
allow a robot to move away from the contacts and stop charging.

Another major problem was the amount of power required to charge a
large number of robots. A complete charge takes approximately three
hours, and each robot can require approximately 0.4 A from a 5 V
power supply. If 100 robots were charged at the same time, the
theoretical current requirement would be

\[
I_{\text{total}} = 100(0.4~\text{A}) = 40~\text{A}.
\]

Therefore, a charging system capable of supporting all 100 robots
simultaneously could require as much as 40 A at 5 V. This made power
distribution an important consideration when designing the charging
system.

To address this problem, 25-port USB charging stations with a
20 A power supply were selected. Each charging station was limited
to 20 Elisa robots. Based on the maximum current requirement of
0.4 A per robot, the expected maximum current for 20 robots is

\[
I_{\text{station}} = 20(0.4~\text{A}) = 8~\text{A}.
\]

This is below the 20 A rating of the charging station and provides
additional capacity instead of operating the power supply near its
maximum rating.

Another possible design was to use the exposed charging contact pins
on the Elisa robots and construct conductive plates that supplied
5 V to multiple robots. However, this approach was not selected.
Although it could reduce the number of individual cables, it would
introduce additional possible failure conditions. For example, a
robot could be placed on the contacts incorrectly, conductive objects
could accidentally contact the exposed power rails, or a new user
could place a robot into the system without understanding the required
orientation. The charging contacts would also need to maintain
reliable physical contact with every robot.

USB cables were selected instead because the charging connection is
more obvious to the user and reduces the amount of exposed powered
conductive material. A user can place a robot in the holder and
connect its USB charging cable without having to understand a
special contact arrangement. This makes the system easier to use
when multiple researchers or new users are working with the robots
and reduces the opportunities for incorrect charging connections.

A cylindrical holder was designed and 3D printed to vertically store
multiple Elisa robots, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:elisa_holder}. The
robots are stacked inside the holder while openings along the side
provide access to USB charging connections. This allows multiple
robots to be charged while keeping the robots and cables organized
in a relatively small area.

Another advantage of the holder is that the openings allow the
charging-status LEDs to remain visible. The charging state can
therefore be checked without removing a robot from the holder.
A change in the charging indication from red to green provides a
simple visual method for determining which robots are still charging
and which are ready for use. This is particularly useful when
preparing a large number of robots for swarm experiments.

